#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087455 :question/id #uuid "6f249bfa-e57b-5784-9da8-d0f9aff213bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21813" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = x^4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670848 :question-generator/id #uuid "9ce90597-a632-53d0-a825-e919fcd5b202" :question-generator/script "generators/89/89da60eff9352611289e3b33adaef5c15c4a340472ea492d7e6427c578d5713d/17592186087868.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be2f153-899d-5fb8-ac16-0ba73180f4fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438112} {:answer/id #uuid "ee580a40-60ca-5953-943d-30dd4b11b6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee580a40-60ca-5953-943d-30dd4b11b6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438113} :answer-field/id #uuid "714a626a-fab4-5f20-a46d-51645fc38dbf" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439085} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec3bd844-d4e8-57ac-ad3b-82c49ec28d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438114} {:answer/id #uuid "fea4c07d-5917-52be-8e1b-5dbb2feaab3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec3bd844-d4e8-57ac-ad3b-82c49ec28d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438114} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee7df271-7391-5bbb-ab29-fda31dd67139" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2xy=4$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439086} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "400d3877-8336-525a-af43-68d24e29fcc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438116} {:answer/id #uuid "f6217fb0-8460-5778-a259-912ec4c56673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6217fb0-8460-5778-a259-912ec4c56673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438117} :answer-field/id #uuid "93800a3e-e593-5d63-9351-6a7fe5d4830b" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2xy=-y$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439087}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "591724df-60a1-5a9d-9f71-6942e86af15b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331601" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=4.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $4$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+2xy=0$; ${y}^{′}+2xy=-y$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2318c126-d9d2-5687-bb8b-918ad14d6156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438178} {:answer/id #uuid "f518ca95-b74e-5582-89dd-63e8f6153da9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2318c126-d9d2-5687-bb8b-918ad14d6156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438178} :answer-field/id #uuid "94ef4ee6-5e52-5c68-8029-62df90194fb8" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439118} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76f99cb4-2678-54dc-a0dd-8a78eba6fbcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438180} {:answer/id #uuid "febffbfc-df33-5888-a072-47e28917a6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76f99cb4-2678-54dc-a0dd-8a78eba6fbcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438180} :answer-field/id #uuid "02c4c8bf-53a2-53cc-ad28-268a12a0d525" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-xy=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439119} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9a725e5-14e8-52a9-acc8-2cbd27718273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438182} {:answer/id #uuid "b294f791-cc47-542e-8756-d3cb4aad5758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b294f791-cc47-542e-8756-d3cb4aad5758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438183} :answer-field/id #uuid "1129b51c-de6b-5813-afae-1fb7f41b9ec6" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439120}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7723c02-822f-5a83-b3af-429d938426d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-331744" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ade60bd6-89a3-5a73-972d-872bbec9ef1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438118} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbc21da-935d-542a-8ab1-73ad0fbda9fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dbc21da-935d-542a-8ab1-73ad0fbda9fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438119} :answer-field/id #uuid "0358c23b-ddd3-5e03-9d10-c033127c1b43" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3efe629-e162-5be1-b2a1-ef4c42a89b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438120} {:answer/id #uuid "a45bc3bf-30fc-52e0-93fe-132d6f93b6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3efe629-e162-5be1-b2a1-ef4c42a89b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438120} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dfb15d0-be1a-5c7b-a003-222aab29fafc" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5508790c-26b9-5808-affc-0f5c42fd9819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438122} {:answer/id #uuid "40377e57-9072-5c7f-9b8d-750b0bacdd09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5508790c-26b9-5808-affc-0f5c42fd9819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438122} :answer-field/id #uuid "118e5a32-cc2b-560d-ac23-990fe960a18c" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439090}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52b69d13-8d12-5e35-b5cc-e11a7f6b65a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331605" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=3x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation has the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff0321f7-e164-5498-b196-4c286eaf7bce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438124} {:answer/id #uuid "ba350085-c43f-5ce4-8a62-63ec28159573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff0321f7-e164-5498-b196-4c286eaf7bce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438124} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d7d5355-19b7-54c4-8b09-d85e8e57729e" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439091} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e337d58-491f-5879-bf5b-e65fb1c3eb1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438126} {:answer/id #uuid "0da375b9-4261-5cc0-b3fe-b7c866eea9c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0da375b9-4261-5cc0-b3fe-b7c866eea9c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438127} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0e14497-143c-5934-8cfa-95c35be4fb08" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439092} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89b67917-bd3d-5f65-9067-a429d1042297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438128} {:answer/id #uuid "98208928-1e1c-5c7d-aa97-3ae58d1c5cfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98208928-1e1c-5c7d-aa97-3ae58d1c5cfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438129} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af9d526-9783-5010-9162-846dfff432a0" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439093}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ed9d4c5-c8ba-5702-acc9-702d6e5cd248" :question/math-academy-id "q-331724" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{2}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2{x}^{3}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y$; ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22b30a22-e8bf-521c-90a6-3f4a193abff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438130} {:answer/id #uuid "21794aaa-14c3-5e8a-bbaa-2761662e74b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21794aaa-14c3-5e8a-bbaa-2761662e74b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438131} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dd5b6aa-8b92-5f0c-ad2c-a55d462aa082" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-xy=-y$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439094} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab3067f1-1415-59b1-8610-cf2333e9d171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438132} {:answer/id #uuid "7a90b93a-6c14-52f2-bdf3-931241711cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a90b93a-6c14-52f2-bdf3-931241711cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438133} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1dfa1a-de10-5f44-9760-65dd587cb80b" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439095} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d659f16-e228-5cec-abc7-98ba7fc32f19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438134} {:answer/id #uuid "5438dd0d-7002-5197-8393-206d7d4887ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5438dd0d-7002-5197-8393-206d7d4887ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438135} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c171570-be09-53d8-bdb3-f8add2aaf918" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-xy=-5y$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439096}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4c450ce-db9f-5997-8c48-e9942b8b8389" :question/math-academy-id "q-331725" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-5y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-5y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+5y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-5)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}-xy=-y$; ${y}^{′}-xy=0$; ${y}^{′}-xy=-5y$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a05adb9-4f73-5867-bae3-ff6175a13e04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438136} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0154cf-98d8-5cc7-9bac-150731181edd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a0154cf-98d8-5cc7-9bac-150731181edd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438137} :answer-field/id #uuid "a41ee71f-fd54-5303-b163-f2032d6801c3" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439097} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29064d13-7994-5b10-9af4-dbca4742bcfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438138} {:answer/id #uuid "8fef5b4b-662f-579a-bd84-e9fad058b00a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29064d13-7994-5b10-9af4-dbca4742bcfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438138} :answer-field/id #uuid "a94578c7-4ce4-5913-a0af-1a5e8cbee912" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439098} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f454d491-4ca7-5ed2-8988-fdbd860e0db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438140} {:answer/id #uuid "b48480a2-0492-52d3-a196-2a8da93df95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b48480a2-0492-52d3-a196-2a8da93df95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438141} :answer-field/id #uuid "03434b70-7877-52d6-9480-68bedbdb7560" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439099}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cb41985-a448-5edf-ac16-5140f9df994f" :question/math-academy-id "q-331727" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y$; ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "614bde2f-3b34-55b7-8979-0bbe0e0e8d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438142} {:answer/id #uuid "2440c20b-4602-5338-a005-b76f98501570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2440c20b-4602-5338-a005-b76f98501570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438143} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1a50845-014d-5d37-b7ad-8ae39954a63e" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-4xy=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439100} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50f411e6-4387-5475-b44a-3ebe176b8d0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438144} {:answer/id #uuid "acaef6e2-a03c-5a66-aae6-11b882603afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acaef6e2-a03c-5a66-aae6-11b882603afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438145} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c7d2e1e-2a32-5f22-89f7-d692d818f8c4" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-4xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439101} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1bd087a-665c-563f-a5fa-648107296f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438146} {:answer/id #uuid "f1acffb4-55b8-551e-b237-9deee744667f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1bd087a-665c-563f-a5fa-648107296f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438146} :answer-field/id #uuid "c735e342-a746-5fcf-8d38-1e9b66bb4050" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-4xy=9$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439102}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70f3dd1b-d16f-5aa1-9eba-10b4e0fd71e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331728" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(4x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=9.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $9$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}-4xy=y$; ${y}^{′}-4xy=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "659f5dad-d96d-572c-bc0e-6839e5d2f979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438148} {:answer/id #uuid "26b77c0c-415c-551c-9af2-e73c88f28745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "659f5dad-d96d-572c-bc0e-6839e5d2f979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438148} :answer-field/id #uuid "8612dfff-4796-54a5-a331-df6cd79613e9" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+7xy=5$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439103} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbc86210-2f25-55e4-979f-8cd8499bd6a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438150} {:answer/id #uuid "4eafb62b-02d1-5dba-aea3-5813048a7402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eafb62b-02d1-5dba-aea3-5813048a7402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438151} :answer-field/id #uuid "581545d9-353d-50b3-8961-7af4bb789164" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+7xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439104} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46b6717f-cd6d-5839-8478-f93130f282fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438152} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc30338-2228-5ddc-91d4-fa268dd71886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdc30338-2228-5ddc-91d4-fa268dd71886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438153} :answer-field/id #uuid "b69443e9-7deb-5890-aee3-96bd29b55c46" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+7xy=-2y$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439105}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8cfe21c-18ac-5f6d-9859-3a5cbe483542" :question/math-academy-id "q-331729" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=5.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $5$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=-2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+7xy & =-2y \\\\ {y}^{′}+7xy+2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(7x+2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+7xy=0$; ${y}^{′}+7xy=-2y$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "667d5f81-9239-59f3-bd64-e30a13824e43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438154} {:answer/id #uuid "320f233a-3b54-548f-96cb-6b5c4aa6ac98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "320f233a-3b54-548f-96cb-6b5c4aa6ac98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438155} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c546fc3-85f8-5495-bac0-790330521078" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+4xy=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439106} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cc51b98-5b0c-5884-b8ee-f552971ac473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438156} {:answer/id #uuid "f499baaa-7017-54d1-957c-4660554066fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cc51b98-5b0c-5884-b8ee-f552971ac473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438156} :answer-field/id #uuid "03137208-0438-51b2-9ef5-6db630037564" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+4xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439107} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "345d36c9-0fc8-53ad-b297-f2466b7f6b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438158} {:answer/id #uuid "749d5e36-3386-5223-bb66-49011d5f7f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "345d36c9-0fc8-53ad-b297-f2466b7f6b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438158} :answer-field/id #uuid "093b0f41-e3f6-5616-abd6-6d38edcb4b50" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+4xy=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439108}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e88e764-3128-53c6-9d2a-d1f58dae12a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331738" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(4x-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+4xy=2$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60013046-6869-55cf-91f7-48420115bedd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438160} {:answer/id #uuid "1722b0bf-64b9-51d2-999e-515e7b90211b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1722b0bf-64b9-51d2-999e-515e7b90211b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438161} :answer-field/id #uuid "a97231ab-1b29-58c9-ac31-9f761a81bcc6" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439109} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "826ce20d-1cce-5586-9508-6cbc2e7afe87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438162} {:answer/id #uuid "795a6de7-fdd5-572b-8fcb-d6f2d8543bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "826ce20d-1cce-5586-9508-6cbc2e7afe87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438162} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9a1df3e-533f-5e3d-84b5-f5af94ba6d6e" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dda6491-25bc-5b4a-9996-e5ade3c17097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438164} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb81709-b7ca-5a51-98e8-419baa95af13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dda6491-25bc-5b4a-9996-e5ade3c17097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438164} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3f50ef3-6ae4-5f6f-855a-f7c193330d33" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439111}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4b9025a-0c2a-511c-b23e-f617ee7031f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331739" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-({x}^{2}+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304942 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8061805c-8cd8-5cc7-bb13-3ad6f9b44f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438166} {:answer/id #uuid "21688abb-cb3b-5a82-a801-8feb6d03c650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8061805c-8cd8-5cc7-bb13-3ad6f9b44f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438166} :answer-field/id #uuid "343bed01-1c21-5af3-bf76-63d75646e463" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2y=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439112} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4441feeb-02b9-5ed8-bcf7-76369fb709ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438168} {:answer/id #uuid "b00c337c-80cb-57cb-9d4a-4a84e893786c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b00c337c-80cb-57cb-9d4a-4a84e893786c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438169} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef31dc0a-a2e6-5f82-be3b-19b7341583d4" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2y=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc31299-2229-5ec9-aa16-a216dd4411f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438170} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb79f69-b06c-55a2-a77c-653ce60b8e57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cc31299-2229-5ec9-aa16-a216dd4411f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438170} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce053aea-15b4-561f-8d1f-f208bfa7025d" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439114}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca0875e1-4252-5a69-bfc7-c43bf6770e82" :question/math-academy-id "q-331742" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+2y=x$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de390821-11f1-5f36-9802-7d4b56d82028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438172} {:answer/id #uuid "d04a28d1-2ea2-50b4-af54-7f274cf2e92c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d04a28d1-2ea2-50b4-af54-7f274cf2e92c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438173} :answer-fiel