estion.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea086a0d-6281-50e8-9859-feb937690d11" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y=2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =2y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y=-4x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=-4x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y=2y$; ${y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y=y$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "885f97b2-8116-577a-b4c8-2fb47506a1a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670820} {:answer/id #uuid "930d61a1-63ff-581c-8958-e9e1eb4da95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "930d61a1-63ff-581c-8958-e9e1eb4da95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670821} :answer-field/id #uuid "17a8d885-5fc2-5eaf-9a04-ecea73a2524b" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y=-4y$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670803} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d186eeb-24d9-5746-81b4-03bdfbb3946c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670805} {:answer/id #uuid "c7dd9cd2-9a9a-53b4-84ad-b91d884d609f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d186eeb-24d9-5746-81b4-03bdfbb3946c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670805} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bff5616-0cc4-5b8a-938e-76f98cb89377" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y=-5$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670807} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f60f433-4104-544d-ba20-53ce8d39c651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670801} {:answer/id #uuid "d140c08d-bd6c-5e13-8631-f0e85f27c412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f60f433-4104-544d-ba20-53ce8d39c651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670801} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8c9ac1a-1072-5be9-af56-7872d02f01e2" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y=3y$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186670839}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa221a93-5c26-51e7-9d03-310306b15d77" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y=-5.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $-5$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y=3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y & =3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y-3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2{x}^{2}-3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y=-4y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y & =-4y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y+4y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2{x}^{2}+4)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y=3y$; ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{2}y=-4y$ only."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}]}
